# One page, one address: https, and no www.
#
# The site answers on four addresses — apex and www, http and https — and served
# the same bytes on every one of them with no redirect anywhere. Two identical
# addresses for a search engine to choose between, and two hosts as far as
# Yandex.Metrika is concerned. The apex over https is the canonical one: it is
# what background.js opens after the extension is installed, and what the
# counter is registered for.
#
# WHY THE SCHEME COMES FROM A HEADER AND NOT FROM %{HTTPS}
#
# TLS is terminated in front of Apache here, so Apache never sees the request as
# an https one. Measured on this host by asking mod_rewrite itself, rather than
# assumed:
#
#                       over http    over https
#   %{HTTPS}            off          off          <- always off, tells nothing
#   %{SERVER_PORT}      not 443      not 443      <- same
#   X-Forwarded-Proto   absent       https        <- the only honest one
#
# So the usual recipe, RewriteCond %{HTTPS} off, would match on an https request
# as well and send it to https again, and again, for ever. Only the header can
# be trusted here.
#
# The test is "not https" rather than "= http" because on a plain request the
# header is not set to anything — it simply is not there.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: the www rule comes first and writes out https itself, so
# http://www reaches the canonical address in one hop rather than two.
RewriteEngine On

# www -> apex, always https
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^www\.(.+)$ [NC]
RewriteRule ^ https://%1%{REQUEST_URI} [R=301,L]

# http -> https
RewriteCond %{HTTP:X-Forwarded-Proto} !=https
RewriteRule ^ https://%{HTTP_HOST}%{REQUEST_URI} [R=301,L]
